                <note rend="inline" type="source">838.51/3713: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Haiti</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Finley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-09-12T19:00:00-04:00">September 12, 1938—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>75. Your 121, September 9, 1 p.m. Please take up this question immediately with
                    the Foreign Minister and reiterate strongly that your Government expects that
                    American products upon importation into Haiti will receive the same customs
                    treatment as that accorded to intrinsically similar products imported from
                    France. If Leger does not agree to extend such treatment, you are authorized in
                    your discretion to take up the matter with President Vincent.</p>
                <p>You may refer to the specific case on which Leger will make his decision
                    tomorrow, provided you are satisfied that the pharmaceutical products in
                    question are in fact intrinsically similar to French products included in
                    Franco-Haitian treaty.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
